var RG = window.RG;

RG.whyIntro = [
  "Why did God make anything at all? The relational gospel begins with a simple answer: <strong>God is love</strong> (1 John 4:8). Before there was a world, there was the Father loving the Son in the Spirit (John 17:24). God did not create because he was lonely, bored or lacking something. He created because love that is already full loves to share itself. Creation is not a machine God built to keep himself busy. It is an overflow of the life and joy that the Father, Son and Spirit already share, opened up so that others could be welcomed in.",
  "That means every part of God’s design is <em>relational</em>. Our bodies, our nerves, our emotions, our families, our work, our rest, even our limits were shaped for communion: with God, with one another, with ourselves and with the rest of creation. Scripture’s first verdict on the human being is not “sinner” but “very good” (Gen 1:31), and its first problem is not a broken rule but loneliness: “It is not good that the man should be alone” (Gen 2:18). God designs from love, and he designs <em>for</em> love. When we ask “why did God make this?”, the answer usually starts here: to carry, express or protect connection.",
  "Every good design can be distorted. Fear, shame and the sense of separation that Scripture traces back to the garden (Gen 3:7-10) bend good things out of shape. Hunger becomes greed, honour becomes vanity, authority becomes control, rest becomes collapse, sexuality becomes consumption. And where a design is distorted, humans often build a <strong>counterfeit</strong>: something that imitates what we were made for without actually giving it. Counterfeits promise the fruit of communion without the vulnerability of communion. They are the reason so many of us feel busy, stimulated and surrounded, yet still hungry.",
  "The good news is that God did not abandon his design. In Jesus, the Son of God took a real human body, a real nervous system, real emotions, a real family, real work and a real death, and he healed humanity from the inside (John 1:14; Heb 2:14-18; 4:15). Early Christian writers like Irenaeus and Athanasius saw the Incarnation as God restoring his image in us by joining himself to us. Christ is the measure of what each design was meant to be: he shows us a human life fully alive in love. And the Spirit is poured into bodies (Rom 8:11; 1 Cor 6:19) to bring that life into every part of us.",
  "This section walks through many areas of life and asks five questions of each: <em>Why</em> did God design it? <em>How</em> does it actually work, including what reputable science can tell us? How does it reflect the Triune God? What counterfeit do we build instead, and how does it become toxic? And how can we live it well in Christ? We will try to keep clear what Scripture says, what Christian tradition has said, what science has found and what is interpretation. Some areas are debated among sincere Christians, and we will say so. Nothing here is medical or clinical advice; where pain runs deep, trusted people and qualified professionals are part of God’s provision too."
];

