    area: "Humans (the image of God)",
    group: "Creation",
    why: "God made humans “in our image, after our likeness” (Gen 1:26) so that there would be creatures who could know him, reflect him and share his life. The plural “let us” has been read by many Christians since the early church as a hint of the Trinity, though Jewish and many modern scholars read it as a royal or heavenly-court plural, so it is debated. Either way, the image is given to humanity “male and female” together (Gen 1:27), which points to a relational image rather than a lonely one.",
    mechanism: "The Hebrew words <em>tselem</em> (image) and <em>demut</em> (likeness) were used in the ancient world for statues that represented a king in his territory. So humans are God’s living representatives, called to rule with care, multiply life and tend the earth (Gen 1:28; 2:15). Christian tradition has located the image in reason, in moral freedom, in relationship and in royal vocation; many today see these as overlapping rather than competing. The New Testament names Christ as “the image of the invisible God” (Col 1:15), so we discover what the image means by looking at him.",
    reflects: "As the Son is the perfect image of the Father, humans are made in and for that image, created to share the Son’s relationship with the Father in the Spirit (Rom 8:29; 2 Cor 3:18). We are icons made for communion.",
    counterfeit: "Seeking worth through achievement, image management or comparison instead of receiving it as a gift from the One whose image we bear.",
    toxic: "The image becomes distorted when people are valued by usefulness, status, ethnicity, gender or productivity, or when we try to become “like God” by grasping rather than receiving (Gen 3:5). This produces both arrogance and shame, and makes it easier to treat others as less than human (James 3:9).",
    healthy: "See every person, including yourself, as a bearer of God’s image whom Christ has joined to himself. Let your identity rest in being beloved, and let your “rule” look like Jesus: serving, protecting and making others flourish.",
    refs: ["Gen 1:26-28", "Gen 5:1-3", "Gen 9:6", "Ps 8:4-6", "Col 1:15", "2 Cor 3:18", "2 Cor 4:4", "Rom 8:29", "James 3:9"],
    science: "",
    scienceUrl: ""
